 high  degree nodes takes too many rounds  in fact it can take up to diameter  number of rounds in the distributed  model  so for high diameter graphs like real  networks that is too expensive  so now i'm going to describe our  distributed algorithm and i'll start  with a very high level description  our distributed algorithms works in the  following way  as in gupta and khan low degree vertices  have priority in entering the mis  on an edge insertion  an endpoint removes itself from the mis  if the edge insertion violates  independence  in this example  one of the endpoints of the insertion  removes itself  after the vertex removes itself its  neighbors are added to the mis if  maximality is violated  low degree neighbors have priority in  adding themselves to the mis  after the low degree neighbors have  added themselves to the mis potentially  a large number of high degree vertices  may need to remove themselves from the  mis  in this example one high degree node  needed to remove itself from the mis  so we remove it  and finally if there are any remaining  high degree vertices that need to enter  the mis they proceed to do so but these  high degree nodes need to determine an  mis in the inducer graph amongst  themselves and we'll talk a bit later  about how they decide which ones will  enter the mis  on an edge deletion  maximality may be violated and so  neighbors are added to the mis if  possible giving priority to low degree  nodes in this example a high degree node  is added to the mis since no additional  low degree nodes may be added  as in the case for insertions there are  potentially many high degree nodes that  may be added or removed from the mis as  a result of a single low degree node  entering or leaving the amis  there are some important things  to consider about the algorithm i just  described so let me mention them now  first edge insertions  or deletions may cause nodes to switch  their degree designation so low degree  nodes may become high degree and vice  versa many times throughout the course  of the entire algorithm over many  updates  then when an edge update causes a low  degree node to enter or leave the mis  the dynamic algorithm of aoss 18 is run  on the low degree node to determine its  set of low degree neighbors to add to  the mis  while several low degree nodes may enter  the mis as a result of a edge update at  most one low degree node ever leaves due  to an update specifically due to an edge  insertion  low degree nodes that enter the mis can  cause many high degree nodes to leave as  we mentioned before  and similarly  low degree nodes that leave the mis can  cause many high degree nodes to enter  while gupta and khan perform a global  restart at every update step among all  high degree nodes to determine the set  that enters or leaves the mis  what we do instead is that we perform a  local restart on the set of high degree  nodes in the local neighborhood only  when needed  and we will give specific details on  this procedure in the next few slides  tying our algorithm back to the  challenges this entire algorithm hinges  on the fact that we need to know which  nodes are high degree and which are no  degree which is a non-trivial hurdle to  overcome and furthermore we need to know  when nodes become high degree from low  degree and vice versa  and we solve the second challenge i  mentioned before by performing a local  restart only when necessary compared to  the gupta anchor algorithm which  performs a global research in the  centralized model among all high degree  nodes after every update step  now we'll give  more details on how we solve challenge  one which is how to determine low degree  high degree designations  to solve challenge one we initialize the  counter pv for every node v which starts  with value 1  assuming that we start with an empty  graph  all vertices are initially low degree  and when the degree of any vertex  exceeds 2pv due to edge insertions  for example vertex v in this diagram  where vertex v  exceeds degree 2.  the vertex then makes itself high degree  the node v then updates pv to equal its  current degree when it becomes low  degree again  this procedure may result in too many  high degree nodes importantly  we don't deal with them now we make high  degree nodes low degree again when we  perform a local restart  and as i mentioned before local restarts  are only performed when we need to  determine which high degree nodes need  to enter the mis  the rest of the procedure then boils  down to solving challenge two which is  determining the set of high degree nodes  that need to enter the mis  suppose in the example the highlighted  yellow nodes are in the mis  on an edge insertion a low degree  neighbor may need to leave the mis in  this example you or his neighbor must  leave amis  so suppose you left the mis then there  may be many high degree neighbors that  want to enter the mis in this in this  example x y and v  no longer have any neighbors in the mis  so they all want to enter but of course  not all of them can enter because there  are some edges connecting them  so they must determine an mis in their  induced neighborhood which would tell  you which ones will be added to the mis  before we run the algorithm for  determining which ones will enter we  must go back and solve challenge 1 again  since some of these quote-unquote  high-degree notes may actually be low  degree  uh recall from before we haven't  actually yet  dealt with these high degree vertices  which have become low degree due to  deletions  so to determine the set of high degree  vertices that have become low degree we  first determine the set of degrees of  all nodes in the one hop neighborhood of  the low degree vertex that we initially  removed from the mis in this example  that vertex is u and the sum of the  degrees of this one half neighborhood is  12.  then any vertex with degree less than  the sum raised to the two thirds becomes  low degree  in this example x and y both have less  than 12 to the two thirds degree so they  become low degree  vertices which became low degree  have priority in joining the mis  and so  both x and y in this diagram join the  mis  suppose instead  x and y remain high degree after doing  our local restart  we run a static distributed mis  algorithm on the induced subgraph of  high degree nodes in the local  neighborhood of u  the specific algorithm we use is the  static algorithm of sensor hello porter  and swartzman which operates in  polylogin rounds on small diameter grass  as you can see in this example this  induced subgraph has small diameter and  in fact the diameters of the subgraphs  we look at in our algorithm are at most  six  specifically their algorithm requires a  leader  and so in our case the low degree node  which removes itself from the mis  initially  is the leader  in this example  you becomes the leader  because their algorithm is deterministic  it requires all the nodes participating  in the algorithm to communicate  information with the leader over several  polylog rounds  this is the reason why  small diameter is important in order to  obtain polylog and total rounds for the  entire algorithm  running their algorithm in this example  we pick v to join the mis  the message complexity of our algorithm  is o of m to the two thirds log squared  and amortized  where m is the average number of edges  over all updates we also show the same  result for maximum number of edges  this message complexity is due to the  fact that low degree vertices have  degree of m to the two thirds  and there are at most of m to the  two-thirds edges in the local high  degree neighborhood  finally this bound is amortized because  the lojo restarts which determine  whether any high degree nodes become low  degree is amortized  the log squared n factor comes from the  number of rounds our algorithm runs  our algorithm requires both log squared  and rounds because cp as 20 the static  algorithm that we use for finding the  mis among the high degree neighbors  requires all log squared and rounds on  constant diameter graphs  as in this example here and as i  mentioned before we only ever run our  algorithm on local subgraphs with  diameter at most six  the amortization in the bound comes from  running aoss 18  on the low degree neighbors and this  algorithm requires of one amortized  rounds  and that concludes the technical portion  of this presentation um so to conclude  with some final thoughts we initialize a  formal study of message efficient  distributed dynamic algorithms we show a  number of results that achieve the grand  price  up two of log squared and factor or  smaller  in obtaining these results we solve  several rather general challenges such  as an unknown number of edges or how to  perform restarts efficiently and locally  there are a number of questions that are  interesting as a result of our study and  we think that these questions will be  interesting directions to explore in  future work  and so these questions include can the  techniques in our paper be generalized  for y class of dynamic distributed  algorithms  can we achieve worst case balance  especially for the number of rounds for  mis  furthermore can we also  get rid of the o of log square and n  factors especially in round complexity  for mis  recall the log squared n factors were a  result of running the static algorithm  so can we perhaps use another technique  to get rid of them  can our algorithms be modified to handle  multiple concurrent updates while  maintaining low message complexity  and finally is there a general purpose  compiler which takes a centralized  dynamic algorithm and outputs a message  efficient distributed dynamic algorithm  so this is a very general question but  we think it would be very interesting if  we can provide such a framework for  turning centralized dynamic algorithms  to dynamic distributed algorithms where  the message complexity equals the update  time  and  we maintain all of one rounds  this is possibly the way to achieve the  grand prize  and with this i conclude our  presentation please check out our paper  and we're happy to take any questions  over email
 

